---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/post-purchase';
const title = "Post-purchase email: from order to successful use";
const desc = "Plan what a buyer needs while waiting, receiving and learning to use an order. Separate service updates from retention asks, and build branches around actual line items and fulfillment state.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should a cross-sell send?",
    "answer": "After the buyer has had a relevant opportunity to receive and use the product, subject to unresolved order problems and message load. Delivery and category context matter more than a fixed day count."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a different shipping address prove a gift order?",
    "answer": "No. Use explicit gift information where available and neutral wording when intent is uncertain. A recipient delivery record does not authorize marketing enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should partial deliveries work?",
    "answer": "Track the relevant item or shipment and distinguish arrived products from outstanding ones. Do not start every usage or review message when the first parcel arrives."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Workflow guide</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Separate order facts from marketing asks</h2>
      <p>Inventory the confirmations, shipping updates and education already sent by the store, fulfillment partner and email platform. Assign one owner for each message. A receipt needs order facts and a support route; a promotional cross-sell has a different purpose and audience.</p>
      <p>Do not rebuild every service notification inside a new marketing flow by default. Document which messages remain with the order system and how preferences apply to the promotional path. Make critical order information available even when a customer leaves marketing.</p>
      <h2>Choose the clock for each message</h2>
      <p>Order placed, fulfilled, shipped and delivered describe different moments. Track the source and reliability of each event. A fulfillment event alone is not proof that a parcel reached the buyer.</p>
      <p>Send preparation instructions before they are needed and usage guidance when the relevant product is likely available. If delivery data is missing, use an explicitly estimated timeline and neutral wording. Define a delayed-parcel branch instead of letting a fixed timer ask for a review of an undelivered order.</p>
      <h2>Build education around line items</h2>
      <p>Map product families to approved setup, care, sizing or activation material. For a mixed basket, combine compatible modules and prioritize the instructions needed first. A bundle may require a single setup sequence rather than one email for each component.</p>
      <p>Version the content against product revisions and link to current instructions. Digital products need access and activation help; equipment may need assembly; apparel may need fit and exchange information. Copy should follow actual product guidance rather than inventing usage or health advice.</p>
      <h2>Handle split, preorder and replacement orders</h2>
      <p>Track fulfillment at the line-item or shipment level when products arrive separately. Explain what shipped and what remains outstanding. Avoid marking the entire purchase as ready for use because one accessory arrived.</p>
      <p>Preorders and backorders need updated expectations when dates change. Replacements and exchanges should receive relevant help without restarting an acquisition-style sequence. Test a cancelled line and a fully refunded order so stale messages do not continue selling the original product.</p>
      <h2>Distinguish the buyer from the user</h2>
      <p>Use explicit gift information where available. A different billing and shipping address is only a clue, not proof of a gift. When intent is unknown, use neutral language and let the buyer find relevant care information.</p>
      <p>Do not enroll a recipient in marketing merely because their delivery address appears on an order. Keep gift pricing private and provide a shareable help link where useful. Subscription starters need setup and account-management guidance, while repeat subscribers may already know the product.</p>
      <h2>Route problems toward resolution</h2>
      <p>Map recurring tracking, access, assembly and fit questions to helpful content and an accessible support route. An open return or delivery problem should change a promotional branch when the data is available. Essential service updates still need an owner.</p>
      <p>Let support see which messages were sent and which instructions the customer received. Do not promise a ticket reduction from adding an email. Measure contacts per comparable order cohort and check whether customers can actually resolve their issue.</p>
      <h2>Schedule review, loyalty and cross-sell asks</h2>
      <p>Allow enough experience with the product before requesting feedback. Different categories and fulfillment conditions need different timing. A review request, referral invitation and cross-sell need not all follow the same order.</p>
      <p>Coordinate these asks with loyalty and replenishment owners. Exclude an already purchased item when it is an irrelevant recommendation, but allow deliberate repeat buying where appropriate. Define message priority from the customer task and active problem, rather than a universal hierarchy or fixed day count.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate contribution over a fair window</h2>
      <p>Track successful activation or setup, support contacts, returns, review submissions and repeat purchase by product family. Use equal follow-up windows so newer cohorts are not compared with fully matured cohorts.</p>
      <p>Attributed repeat revenue is not proof of incremental gain. Compare a defined change against a suitable control where feasible. Use net sales after refunds and discounts, then subtract relevant product, fulfillment, incentive and operating costs without subtracting the same discount twice.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate the app using an order lifecycle</h2>
      <p>Ask the candidate to process a mixed basket, a partial shipment, a delayed parcel, an explicit gift, a refund and a subscription start. Require a clear preview of branch decisions and a way to pause the promotional path. These are demonstration requests, not tests performed for this guide.</p>
      <p>Include delivery-data integration, content maintenance, contact or send charges and support workload in the comparison. A gap in one delivery event may call for an integration fix rather than replacement of the whole email stack.</p>
      <h2>Release with a replayable order record</h2>
      <p>Create controlled orders with expected branch outcomes. Verify received content, links and destination access. Replay the same event to check that it does not duplicate a message; then change fulfillment or refund state while a message is queued.</p>
      <p>Keep the prior sender paused before enabling its equivalent. Preserve event history and a rollback owner. Review missing events and customer replies after release, and refresh instructions when products change rather than following an arbitrary migration season.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related workflows">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">Gift orders →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">Reviews and UGC →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment →</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
